 "takeaways": [
  "Most of what you’ll see is Gothic, Renaissance or Baroque, or a 19th-century “neo” revival of one of them. Learn those three and every old town starts to make sense.",
  "Old towns are built for walking; cars are an afterthought. It’s the opposite of the US.",
  "Many older buildings have no lift, so people of every age climb stairs every day, and it shows.",
  "Milk and butter taste noticeably better than at home.",
  "Water can cost more than beer. In Krakow it even carried 23% tax.",
  "Beer is mostly mild and pale, made for drinking a lot of it.",
  "Public toilets usually cost a coin or two; carry change.",
  "Every bathroom in every apartment has its own quirk.",
  "The churches everywhere are stunning, and most have a giant pipe organ.",
  "Leave slack in every city to wander, shop, eat and watch. Some of our best moments weren’t planned."
 ],
 "notes": [
  {
   "title": "Where I learnt the most history",
   "items": [
    {
     "point": "The Rynek Underground Museum in Krakow changed how I saw the map.",
     "detail": "It sits under the main square. Its maps put Krakow at the centre of medieval Europe’s trade routes: a Europe-centred view of the world that was new to me."
    },
    {
     "point": "Buda Castle is dense with history, especially the invasions.",
     "detail": "The Budapest History Museum inside the castle walks through the armies that came through Hungary, starting with the Romans, when Budapest was Aquincum on the empire’s frontier."
    },
    {
     "point": "Auschwitz-Birkenau is sobering, and worth the time.",
     "detail": "Book a slot well ahead."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Driving and renting",
   "items": [
    {
     "point": "One car for the whole trip, and the one-way fee was most of the cost.",
     "detail": "We rented from Budget, picked up in Prague and dropped off in Venice. Picking up and dropping off in the same city is far cheaper, if your route allows it."
    },
    {
     "point": "Tell the rental company every country you’ll drive through.",
     "detail": "Crossing borders needs their permission and often a cross-border fee. Without it the insurance and breakdown cover can be void."
    },
    {
     "point": "Buy an e-vignette before each country’s motorways.",
     "detail": "Czechia, Slovakia, Hungary, Austria and Slovenia need one, tied to your number plate; we bought each online as we were about to cross the border. Poland and Italy charge tolls on some motorways instead. In Austria a digital vignette bought online only becomes valid 18 days after purchase, so buy it early or get a sticker at a petrol station."
    },
    {
     "point": "Parking is often extra at hotels and apartments.",
     "detail": "Check before you book if you’re driving."
    },
    {
     "point": "The drives are part of the trip.",
     "detail": "The most beautiful: the Dolomites passes on day 13, Zakopane to Budapest through the Slovak Tatras on day 6, and up to the Vršič Pass on day 11."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Where we stayed",
   "items": [
    {
     "point": "We stayed in a mix of Airbnbs and apartments from Booking.com.",
     "detail": "Kitchens and showers were small by American standards almost everywhere, and not every place had laundry, so check the listing if you need it."
    },
    {
     "point": "Check the stairs before you book.",
     "detail": "Our Airbnb in Zakopane was in the attic and extremely cramped, and the one in Kranjska Gora had a very narrow, steep staircase. Many older houses have no lift, so with big bags, look for stairs and ceiling height in the listing photos."
    }
